## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end integration of AWS AI services with a clean event-driven pattern, including common NLU tasks (sentiment, themes) and a custom-entities layer for domain knowledge. It also probes data handling, privacy, and operational trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture with S3 -> Lambda
- Amazon Comprehend: sentiment, key phrase/theme extraction
- Comprehend Custom Entities for product-domain data
- DynamoDB upsert semantics and partitioning strategy
- Data privacy: encryption (KMS), access logs, least privilege
- Reliability: idempotency, retries, error handling
- Cost: batching, thresholds, per-request pricing

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend();
const dynamo = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const rec = event.Records[0];
  const bucket = rec.s3.bucket.name;
  const key = decodeURIComponent(rec.s3.object.key.replace(/\+/g, ' '));
  const obj = await s3.getObject({Bucket: bucket, Key: key}).promise();
  const data = JSON.parse(obj.Body.toString('utf-8'));
  const text = data.text;
  const lang = data.language || 'en';

  const sentiment = (await comprehend.detectSentiment({Text: text, LanguageCode: lang}).promise()).Sentiment;
  const themes = (await comprehend.detectEntities({Text: text, LanguageCode: lang}).promise()).Entities.map(e => e.Type);

  await dynamo.put({
    TableName: 'FeedbackIndex',
    Item: {
      feedbackId: data.feedbackId,
      customerId: data.customerId,
      language: lang,
      sentiment,
      themes
    }
  }).promise();

  // Optionally write summary/output to S3 here
  return {status: 'ok'};
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ensure idempotency across retries and duplicate events?
- How would you extend this for multilingual feedback and dynamic entity schemas with evolving product vocabularies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a simple yet complete event-driven pipeline using Rekognition, S3, Lambda, and DynamoDB, while accounting for privacy and failure handling.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event triggers and Lambda integration
- Rekognition DetectLabels and SafeSearch usage
- Writing reports to S3 and upserting DynamoDB records
- IAM least-privilege roles and policy scoping
- Error handling with DLQ and idempotency, privacy controls

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const rekognition = new AWS.Rekognition();
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const dynamodb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const bucket = event.Records[0].s3.bucket.name;
  const key = decodeURIComponent(event.Records[0].s3.object.key.replace(/\+/g, ' '));
  const image = { Image: { S3Object: { Bucket: bucket, Name: key } } };
  const resp = await rekognition.detectLabels({ ...image, MaxLabels: 5 }).promise();
  const labels = resp.Labels.map(l => l.Name);
  const safeFlag = resp.Adult?.Value === false && resp.Violence?.Value === false;
  const report = { objectKey: key, safeFlag, topLabel: labels[0], labels };
  await s3.putObject({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key + '.report.json', Body: JSON.stringify(report) }).promise();
  await dynamodb.put({ TableName: 'ProfileModeration', Item: { userId: '<USER_ID>', objectKey: key, safeFlag, topLabel: labels[0] } }).promise();
  return {};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle mislabeled content or false positives?
- How would you scale to millions of uploads with idempotent processing?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to design an end-to-end, event-driven image analytics pipeline in AWS using beginner-friendly services (S3, Lambda, Rekognition, DynamoDB) with real-world concerns like privacy, cost, and tenancy.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven flow triggered by S3 PUT
- Rekognition detectLabels with top N labels
- Lightweight color palette extraction in Lambda
- Descriptor JSON stored in S3 and DynamoDB upsert
- IAM least privilege, DLQ/backoff, cost awareness

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js Lambda pseudo
const rek = new AWS.Rekognition();
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (evt) => {
  const key = evt.Records[0].s3.object.key;
  const bucket = evt.Records[0].s3.bucket.name;
  const img = await s3.getObject({Bucket:bucket,Key:key}).promise();
  const labels = await rek.detectLabels({Image:{Bytes: img.Body}, MaxLabels:3, MinConfidence: 60}).promise();
  const colors = extractColors(img.Body); // simple utility
  const descriptor = { imageKey:key, labels: labels.Labels.map(l=>l.Name), colors, timestamp: Date.now(), facesDetected: labels.Labels.some(l=>l.Name==='Face') };
  await s3.putObject({Bucket: bucket, Key: key + '.json', Body: JSON.stringify(descriptor)}).promise();
  await ddb.update({TableName:'ProductImages',Key:{productId: determineProductId(key)}, UpdateExpression:'SET labels=:l, colors=:c, imageKey=:k, privacyFlag=:p', ExpressionAttributeValues:{':l':descriptor.labels, ':c':descriptor.colors, ':k':key, ':p':false}}).promise();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate labels and handle false positives? 
- How would you extend for multi-tenant isolation and cost controls?
